     "body": "<p>I setup two Fedora instances with legacyfs and Journaling to keep them in sync. When I ingested an object with a managed datastream the layout on the file system is as follows:</p>\n\n<p> Directory layout:</p>\n\n<p>/usr/local/fedora/data/datastreams/2014/1018/20/48 - primary<br/>\n/usr/local/fedora/data/datastreams/2014/1018/20/53 - secondary</p>\n\n<p>Object dir:</p>\n\n<p>/usr/local/fedora/data/objects/2014/1003/23/34/llgc-id_1 - primary<br/>\n/usr/local/fedora/data/objects/2014/1003/23/39/llgc-id_1 - secondary</p>\n\n<p>i.e. the directory and file layout is different between the primary and secondary. Does this break the Repository Equivalence rule:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://wiki.duraspace.org/display/FEDORA37/Journaling#Journaling-intro-equiv\" class=\"external-link\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">https://wiki.duraspace.org/display/FEDORA37/Journaling#Journaling-intro-equiv</a></p>\n\n<p>It may not be a big issue but if you are only backing up the secondary (this may be the case if you can't do a hot backup on the primary) then you can't restore the primary database as the paths would be different on the secondary. I can't think of many other situations where this would be a problem. </p>",
     "created": "26/Oct/14 10:06 PM",
     "id": "12649",
     "jsdPublic": true,
